A parent table is rebuilt while a child's rows refer to it with ON DELETE
CASCADE. Foreign keys are off through the migration, so dropping the old
parent cascades nowhere: every child row stays.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE review (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, dish_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES dish (id) ON DELETE CASCADE, words TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup'), (2, 'bread');
INSERT INTO review VALUES (1, 1, 'hot'), (2, 2, 'warm');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE review (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, dish_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES dish (id) ON DELETE CASCADE, words TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name") SELECT "id", "name" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"
